issing evidence on stress resilience, decision-making under pressure, crew cooperation, professional communication, and is not ready to vote.\n\nWe as Agent 3 need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing discussion, using explicit facts, and state our current vote.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, unfriendly, eats unhealthily. Also we have no explicit evidence on stress resilience, decision-making under pressure, crew cooperation, professional communication. For other candidates we have facts.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We could either support Candidate A, or suggest another candidate. We need to bring in evidence for other candidates perhaps to compare.\n\nWe have facts for B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone. For C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, puts concern for others above everything, excellent attention skills. For D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, considered moody, regarded as a loner.\n\nWe need to address missing criteria for A, and perhaps bring up B's stress handling (we have that) and B's communication concerns. Also C's decision making quickly (decision quality) and attention skills, but communication issues. D's problem solving and responsibility, but moody/loner.\n\nWe need to argue which candidate best fits overall. Since A lacks evidence on key criteria, maybe we should shift to another candidate with more evidence. B has stress handling, but communication tone issues. C has quick correct decisions, excellent attention, but communication difficulty and egocentric. D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but moody/loner.\n\nWe need to weigh. For long-distance pilot, stress resilience is crucial. B has explicit \"handles stress very well\". Also B is very conscientious (reliability). However B sometimes adopts wrong tone, pretentious. That could affect crew cooperation. But we have no evidence on B's decision-making under pressure. D responds to unexpected events adequately (some decision-making). D also takes responsibility seriously. But D is moody and loner, which may affect crew cooperation.\n\nC makes correct decisions quickly (decision quality), excellent attention, puts concern for others above everything (good crew cooperation?), but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). The \"puts concern for others above everything\" suggests good crew cooperation, but difficulty communicating may hinder.\n\nWe need to decide. Perhaps Candidate C's quick correct decisions and excellent attention are strong for safety, and his concern for others suggests good crew cooperation, despite communication difficulty. But egocentric and unwillingness to further education are concerns.\n\nCandidate B's stress handling is important, but communication tone issues may be serious.\n\nGiven the missing evidence for A, we might shift to Candidate C as a balanced alternative: decision quality, attention, concern for others, but we need to address communication difficulty.\n\nWe could ask for more info on B's decision-making under pressure, or C's stress resilience. But we can also make an argument now.\n\nWe need to produce a public message with a vote. Let's decide to vote for Candidate C, explaining that while A lacks evidence on key criteria, C demonstrates decision quality, attention, and crew concern, which outweigh his communication issues.\n\nWe must reference prior discussion: agree with Agent 2 that missing evidence for A is problematic. Provide evidence for C: quick correct decisions, excellent attention, puts concern for others above everything. Also note concerns: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Argue that his strengths align with core criteria (decision quality, attention, crew cooperation) and that communication can be trained.\n\nThus vote for Candidate C.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: \"can make correct decisions quickly\", \"excellent attention skills\", \"puts concern for others above everything\". Also mention concerns: \"difficulty communicating ideas\", \"regarded as egocentric\", \"not very willing to further his education\". Then argue.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
